.TH TCPSERVE 1
.SH NAME
tcpserve \- accept one TCP connection and answer it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tcpserve
[
.I address
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tcpserve
announces on
.I address
\(em
.B tcp!*!6666
by default \(em waits for one connection, writes a line to it and
exits.  It exists to test the half of the network stack that nothing
else in the kernel root uses.
.PP
Every command written for the cloud so far dials out:
.IR hget (1)
and
.IR gcs (1)
make connections,
.IR cloudfs (4)
reads a service, and
.IR cloudinit (1)
takes a lease.  None of them listens, so
.B announce
and
.B accept
were never exercised on a native kernel at all.  They work: run under
QEMU with a forwarded port, a client on the host connects and reads
.BR INFERNO-ACCEPTED-YOUR-CONNECTION .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/tcpserve.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styxlisten (1),
.IR dial (2)
.SH BUGS
One connection, then it exits.  That is the point of it; anything that
wants to serve should use
.IR styxlisten (1).
